From: bigjakk <6015188+bigjakk@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 12:00:00 -0500
Subject: kcc: [NoAllocDirectCall] on 8 more hot WebGL methods (V8 fast API calls)

Build-time only (IDL); no runtime flag, no C++ change.

Stock state at 156.0.8078.100
-----------------------------
[NoAllocDirectCall] makes the bindings generator emit, next to the normal
FunctionCallbackInfo callback, a C function that TurboFan-optimised code
calls directly (v8::CFunction registered through
FunctionTemplate::NewWithCFunctionOverloads in
platform/bindings/idl_member_installer.cc).

webgl_rendering_context_base.idl has it on (42 declarations): bindBuffer,
bindFramebuffer, bindTexture, clear, clearColor, clearDepth, clearStencil,
colorMask, disableVertexAttribArray, drawArrays, drawElements,
enableVertexAttribArray, scissor, uniform{1,2,3,4}{f,i},
uniform{1,2,3,4}{f,i}v, uniformMatrix{2,3,4}fv, vertexAttrib{1,2,3,4}f,
vertexAttrib{1,2,3,4}fv, vertexAttribPointer, viewport.
webgl2_rendering_context_base.idl has it on (28): the
uniform{1,2,3,4}{f,i,ui}v and uniformMatrix{2,3,4,2x3,3x2,2x4,4x2,3x4,4x3}fv
overloads, vertexAttribI4iv, vertexAttribI4uiv, drawBuffers,
clearBuffer{iv,uiv,fv,fi}.
It is absent from activeTexture, enable, disable, useProgram (WebGL1 base)
and bindVertexArray, drawArraysInstanced, drawElementsInstanced,
vertexAttribDivisor (WebGL2 base). three.js calls bindVertexArray,
useProgram, activeTexture and drawElements[Instanced] per draw, and
bindVertexArray was the most expensive single binding in the game trace.

The contract at this tag (differs from the task description)
------------------------------------------------------------
The "no allocation" contract no longer exists in this tree:
- platform/bindings/no_alloc_direct_call_host.{h,cc} do not exist; there
  is no NoAllocDirectCallHost, no IsInFastMode, no deferrable-action
  mechanism anywhere under third_party/blink.
- bindings/IDLExtendedAttributes.md, [NoAllocDirectCall]: "the NoAlloc
  portion of the name is historical only, as nowadays it is allowed to
  allocate memory, and it is also allowed to throw exceptions and to call
  back to JavaScript." The only stated requirement is that all overloads
  with the same argument count are annotated consistently (asserted in
  web_idl/idl_compiler.py).
- V8 15.6 agrees (src/compiler/turboshaft/fast-api-call-lowering-reducer.h):
  the C call is emitted with a lazy frame state (kNeedsFrameState,
  CanThrow), the current context is stored into the isolate before the
  call, and isolate->exception is checked and propagated after it. (The
  header comment in include/v8-fast-api-calls.h still says "should not
  allocate", but FastApiCallbackOptions has no fallback field any more.)
- The already-annotated methods confirm it: bindBuffer, drawArrays and
  drawElements call SynthesizeGLError() directly, which allocates a
  ConsoleMessage on the Oilpan heap, calls AddConsoleMessage and
  probe::DidFireWebGLError, and they write Member<> fields, all with no
  deferral.
So a wrong annotation is no longer a memory-safety bug by itself. What is
left to check is what the generated fast callback does differently from
the slow one.

What the generator emits (bind_gen/interface.py,
make_no_alloc_direct_call_callback_def)
- Signature: (v8::Local<v8::Object> receiver, args...,
  v8::FastApiCallbackOptions&). Numeric and boolean IDL types become the
  same C++ value types the slow path produces (unsigned long -> uint32_t,
  long -> int32_t, long long -> int64_t). Interface types, nullable or not,
  are passed as v8::Local<v8::Value> and converted inside the callback with
  the same NativeValueTraits<IDLNullable<T>>::ArgumentValue() call as the
  slow path, under a HandleScope and a local ExceptionState; a wrong type
  throws the same TypeError and returns. So "WebGLVertexArrayObject?" and
  "WebGLProgram?" are handled exactly like the precedent "WebGLBuffer?".
- Receiver: ToWrappableUnsafe(), relying on V8 having checked the receiver
  map against the template signature (JSCallReducer::ReduceCallApiFunction
  does not inline otherwise). Same as every precedent.
- It then calls the same C++ member with the same argument expressions.
- It skips, relative to the slow callback: the RuntimeCallStats scope and
  BLINK_BINDINGS_TRACE_EVENT (both compiled out unless special build flags
  are set), the argument-count check (V8 only picks a C function whose
  parameter count equals the argument count at the call site,
  fast_api_call::GetFastApiCallTarget), and the [MeasureAs], [DeprecateAs],
  [LogActivity], [CEReactions], [CallWith], [RaisesException] steps. None
  of the eight operations carries any extended attribute, so nothing is
  lost.
- V8 uses the C function only when a numeric argument is already a Number
  (CheckedNumberAsWord32 / CheckedSigned64AsWord64); anything else
  deoptimises to the slow callback, so WebIDL conversion semantics are
  unchanged.

Per-method audit (GL-backed WebGLRenderingContextBase and
WebGL2RenderingContextBase)
- bindVertexArray(WebGLVertexArrayObject?): ANNOTATED. isContextLost ->
  ValidateNullableWebGLObject (same helper as bindBuffer) ->
  BindVertexArrayOES -> SetHasEverBeenBound -> SetBoundVertexArrayObject
  (one Member<> write). A subset of what bindBuffer does.
- useProgram(WebGLProgram?): ANNOTATED. Virtual; the WebGL2 override
  checks transform feedback state and then calls the base. Base:
  ValidateNullableWebGLObject, WebGLProgram::LinkStatus (may issue one
  synchronous GetProgramiv the first time after a link; plain GL, no V8),
  WebGLObject::OnDetached (may DeleteProgram), a Member<> write,
  OnAttached. Virtual dispatch from a fast callback is already exercised
  by the annotated bindFramebuffer.
- activeTexture(GLenum): ANNOTATED. Range check, SynthesizeGLError on
  failure, one integer write, ActiveTexture.
- enable(GLenum), disable(GLenum): ANNOTATED. ValidateCapability (WebGL2
  override plus base; ExtensionEnabled lookups, SynthesizeGLError), bool
  writes, ApplyDepthAndStencilTest (reads framebuffer / drawing buffer
  state, then Enable/Disable).
- drawArraysInstanced, drawElementsInstanced: ANNOTATED. Identical to the
  annotated drawArrays / drawElements (ValidateDrawArrays /
  ValidateDrawElements + DrawWrapper) except for the GL entry point. The
  GLintptr (int64_t) argument has the drawElements precedent.
- vertexAttribDivisor(GLuint, GLuint): ANNOTATED. Same shape as the
  annotated enableVertexAttribArray.
Rejected: none. No C++ change was needed for any of them.

Neighbours
- None of the eight names is declared a second time in the other mixin or
  in a partial interface, so no overload group changes (the WebGL1
  extension variants drawArraysInstancedANGLE, vertexAttribDivisorANGLE and
  bindVertexArrayOES live on extension objects and are untouched).
- The two mixins are also included by WebGLRenderingContextWebGPU and
  WebGL2RenderingContextWebGPU (RuntimeEnabled=WebGLOnWebGPU, no status,
  i.e. off). They get the same fast callbacks. Their implementations in
  webgl_rendering_context_webgpu_base.cc were read too: activeTexture,
  enable, disable, useProgram and vertexAttribDivisor forward to driver_gl_
  with the same ValidateNullableObject / RETURN_IF_GL_ERROR helpers as the
  annotated bindBuffer there; bindVertexArray, drawArraysInstanced and
  drawElementsInstanced are NOTIMPLEMENTED(). Nothing new.
- modules/webgpu IDL is separate and untouched. There is no webgl2_compute
  context at this tag.

A/B
- Per patch: build with and without this commit.
- At runtime, --js-flags=--no-turbo-fast-api-calls turns off all fast API
  calls (the pre-existing ones too); useful as a kill switch and to bound
  the total gain, not to isolate this patch.
- Measure: FPS / frame time in a scene with many draw calls; renderer
  main-thread samples under the V8 API-callback builtin
  (Builtins_CallApiCallbackOptimized*) for these methods should move to the
  direct C++ frames.

Platforms: all. On 32-bit targets V8 does not use C functions with 64-bit
parameters (drawElementsInstanced), as for drawElements today.

Not verified: nothing was generated or compiled here. The fast path is
taken only from TurboFan code, so the gain depends on the call sites being
optimised and monomorphic.

